#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd14a52-8c49-563a-85b2-8d3e4a3f4cff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "4aae1963-085e-5d98-a22e-5d475f709769" :question/math-academy-id "e-8184" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n1. $P(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2x_3+x_3^2$\n2. $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$, where $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_3^2-2x_1^2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\n). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3$.\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/id #uuid "e0b3629c-0f62-56ef-9e7e-f811958bf36d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8185" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$4$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9([x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}] - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 9x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 - x 2; y_{2} = x_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 3D-Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f889b145-edc7-5721-ab60-c3395b86e4b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8186" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n${y_{1} = [math]\\phantom{x_1+x_2-2x_3}[/math]; y_{2} = [math]\\phantom{x_2-3x_3}[/math]; y_{3} = x_{3}$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3})) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3}) + (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 4x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2$, factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2$, and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - ([x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3} + 9x_{3}^{2}] - 9x_{3}^{2}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + 9x_{3}^{2} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 + x 2 - 2 x 3; y_{2} = x 2 - 3 x 3; y_{3} = x_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b707a638-f8ba-5379-80ae-80d18efc4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} + 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160909} {:answer/id #uuid "d316df5f-eb56-540a-90b2-a38e88608f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $x_{1} + x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160910} {:answer/id #uuid "33338ce3-eac4-5358-ac52-9630cd86cd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $x_{1} + 2x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160911} {:answer/id #uuid "527b2681-2d12-58c6-a1a8-faebae067e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} - 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160912} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4ba19c-3574-561f-9b4c-19b852cb529a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $x_{1} - 2x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b707a638-f8ba-5379-80ae-80d18efc4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $x_{1} + 3x_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160909} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcfe45b-dbad-5a1c-86a5-77078d6e137b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160908}] :question/id #uuid "ca81ca2d-2a11-5281-bdfb-66b5a7ca3f2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94142" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = 3x_{1}^{2} + 29x_{2}^{2} + 18x_{1}x_{2}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$3y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} + 3x_{2}; y_{2} = x_{2}$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?"}]}
